# Windows PowerShell 5.1 is the interpreter these scripts run under, and it has
# two encoding behaviours that bite silently:
#
#   * a .ps1 without a UTF-8 BOM is decoded as the system ANSI codepage — cp949
#     on a Korean Windows — so any non-ASCII character (an em dash in a comment,
#     a Korean string literal) turns into mojibake that can break the parse in
#     ways that look like a C# or a syntax error, nowhere near the real cause;
#   * a here-string (`@"` ... `"@`) only terminates when the file has CRLF
#     endings.
#
# The scripts carry a BOM and avoid here-strings. Pin the endings so a checkout
# on another machine cannot reintroduce the second problem.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
